 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Full Delaunay recomputation is $O(n \log n)$ or worse depending on the algorithm, where $n$ is the number of vertices. Incremental updates are $O(k)$ where $k$ is the number of local flips, but can degrade if many flips are needed.
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(n)$ for storing vertices and tetrahedra. Hybrid rendering triples the memory for face incidence and hash maps [S2].
-- **Measured Performance**: On a scene with 1.2 million tetrahedra, rendering takes 19 ms, Instant-NGP query takes 13 ms, backwards pass takes 143 ms, and Delaunay triangulation computation takes 92 ms [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Balances accuracy and speed by avoiding frequent full recomputations. Incremental updates are faster than full recomputation.
-- **Limitations**: Incremental updates can fail if vertices move too far. Nearly cospherical points can cause numerical issues. Full recomputation is slow.
-- **When to Use**: When vertex positions change slowly during optimization, and full recomputation is too expensive.
-- **When Not to Use**: When vertices move rapidly or unpredictably, or when high precision is required and incremental updates are unstable.
+- **Time Complexity:** Full Delaunay triangulation is $O(N \log N)$ or worse depending on the algorithm, where $N$ is the number of vertices. Incremental updates are local but require checking neighbors.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing vertices and tetrahedra. Hybrid rendering triples the face hash map and DAG storage [S2].
+- **Measured Performance:** On a scene with 1.2 million tetrahedra: 19 ms rendering, 13 ms instant-NGP query, 143 ms backwards pass, 92 ms Delaunay triangulation computation [S1, unofficial].
+- **Scaling:** The periodic update strategy (every 10 iterations) allows for efficient GPU acceleration by amortizing the cost of triangulation over multiple rendering steps [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Maintains geometric validity during optimization; efficient enough for real-time or near-real-time rendering when updates are periodic.
+- **Limitations:** Numerical instability due to nearly cospherical points; full recomputation is expensive.
+- **When to use:** When optimizing vertex positions in a tetrahedral mesh for volume rendering.
+- **When not to use:** When static geometry is sufficient, or when real-time performance is critical and periodic updates are too slow.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive Delaunay updates is rooted in the need to maintain valid tetrahedral meshes during dynamic processes such as optimization in neural rendering. The specific application to Radiance Meshes was introduced in "Radiance Meshes for Volumetric Reconstruction" [S1, unofficial]. The use of Pachner moves for Delaunay maintenance is a standard computational geometry technique [S1, unofficial]. Hybrid rendering with multi-resolution meshes was explored in "Segment-based tetrahedral meshing and rendering" [S2]. Delaunay cover refinement is a classic technique for mesh generation [S3, unofficial].
+The concept of adaptive Delaunay update is rooted in the need to maintain mesh quality during dynamic optimization processes in computer graphics. The specific application to volume rendering with Radiance Meshes was introduced in the paper "Radiance Meshes for Volumetric Reconstruction" [S1, unofficial]. The technique of using Pachner moves for Delaunay refinement is a standard computational geometry technique [S1, unofficial]. The hybrid rendering approach with multi-resolution meshes was proposed in "Segment-based tetrahedral meshing and rendering" [S2].
 
